<BaseLayout title="Independence Break in the Texas Triangle — Huanyu Chen"
  description="A Fourth of July long weekend in 2023: fly into Austin, then drive San Antonio, Fort Worth, Dallas and Houston, ending under a space shuttle named Independence.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · July 2023</p>
  <h1>Independence Break in the Texas Triangle</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Five Texas cities in four days over the Fourth of July, in a car far too red for us.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>5 days · June 30 – July 4, 2023</strong><br />
    Los Angeles → Austin → San Antonio → Austin → Fort Worth → Dallas → Richardson → Houston → IAH<br />
    roughly 750 miles driven · four nights in three cities · one tray of brisket and one table of dim sum · one bright red Dodge Charger · one space shuttle named <em>Independence</em>, visited on Independence Day
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-triangle.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-triangle.jpg'} alt="Map of the trip: Austin, a round trip to San Antonio, then north to Fort Worth and Dallas, and south to Houston and its airport." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>The whole loop: Austin and San Antonio, up to Fort Worth and Dallas, down to Houston.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Geographers have a name for the part of Texas where most Texans live: the <strong>Texas Triangle</strong>. Its
    corners are Dallas–Fort Worth in the north, Houston in the southeast and San Antonio in the southwest, with Austin
    sitting on the western edge between them. Three interstates trace its sides: I-35 up the west, I-45 down the east,
    and I-10 along the bottom. It is a triangle you can drive around in a long weekend, if you don't mind a lot of
    highway, and the Fourth of July in 2023 gave us exactly one long weekend.
  </p>
  <p>
    So we flew into Austin on a Friday night, rented a car, and drove the triangle in four days, ending at Houston's
    airport on the evening of the Fourth. As with my other trip posts, everything here is reconstructed from the camera
    roll: where each photo was taken and when. Where the phone recorded nothing, I've left the gap alone rather than
    fill it with a guess. And where a place has a story worth knowing, I've added a little of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Prologue: a late flight and a red car</h2>
  <p>
    The trip began at LAX a little after eight on a Friday evening, June 30th, at a gate full of people starting the
    same long weekend. Los Angeles to Austin is a short flight that eats two time zones, so it was nearly one in the
    morning, Central Time, when the lights of the northwest Austin suburbs slid under the wing. Austin's baggage claim
    at 1:27 a.m. was as crowded as a Saturday afternoon. Nobody, it seems, wanted to waste a minute of the holiday.
  </p>
  <p>
    We slept near the airport and came back the next day to the rental garage, where the car waiting for us was a
    <strong>Dodge Charger</strong> in a shade of red that can only be described as "loud". It looked like the right car
    for Texas, and it got photographed from every angle before it moved an inch.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'charger.jpg'} alt="A bright red Dodge Charger parked in a rental-car garage at the Austin airport." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The rental, Austin airport, July 1st. Very red.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 1: Austin, then San Antonio</h2>
  <p>
    Austin in July feels like walking into a warm bath. That first afternoon the sky was the classic Central Texas
    summer sky: deep blue, stacked with big, flat-bottomed cumulus clouds, the kind that build all afternoon in the heat
    and humidity, so that by mid-afternoon the whole sky looks like a fleet of white ships riding at anchor.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0701.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0701.jpg'} alt="Map of the route: Day 1: Austin airport, Terry Black's Barbecue, the Cathedral of Junk, UT Austin, and San Antonio's River Walk." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 1: barbecue, junk, a tower, and a river walk.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    We came in from the airport on TX-71, under the big green signs for I-35, which points one way to San Antonio and
    the other to Waco. Then we turned up <strong>South First Street</strong>, where the downtown towers rise ahead
    through a tangle of power lines and live oaks, the clouds sitting on top of them like a second skyline.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'austin-i35-signs.jpg'} alt="Highway signs over a Texas freeway pointing to I-35 South San Antonio and I-35 North Waco under a sky of cumulus clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>San Antonio one way, Waco the other.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'austin-south-first.jpg'} alt="Downtown Austin towers seen through power lines and live oaks from South First Street." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown Austin from South First Street.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Lunch, Texas style</h3>
  <p>
    The first proper stop was lunch, and in Austin there is really only one category of lunch to begin with.
    <strong>Terry Black's Barbecue</strong> on Barton Springs Road is run by a branch of the Black family, whose
    barbecue in Lockhart, half an hour south, goes back to 1932. Central Texas barbecue is about as simple as cooking
    gets: beef, salt, pepper, post-oak smoke and a great many hours. You order at a counter by the pound, and it comes on
    butcher paper on a plastic tray.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ours had slices of brisket with a black, peppery bark and a pink smoke ring, a beef rib with a bone the size of a
    small club, a couple of pork ribs, green beans, mac and cheese, rice and a pile of pickle chips, along with a Shiner
    Bock (brewed in a small Texas town of the same name since 1913) and a Bud Light for balance. It came to a wooden table
    under the trees, and it looked exactly like the first meal in Texas should look.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'terry-blacks.jpg'} alt="A red tray of brisket, a beef rib and pork ribs on butcher paper with green beans, mac and cheese and rice, beside a Shiner Bock, a Bud Light and a red Terry Black's cup." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Terry Black's, Barton Springs Road.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The Cathedral of Junk</h3>
  <p>
    After lunch we went looking for the weirdest thing in a city whose unofficial motto is "Keep Austin Weird". In a
    backyard in a quiet South Austin neighborhood stands the <strong>Cathedral of Junk</strong>. Vince Hannemann started
    building it in 1988 and has been adding to it ever since. It is a multi-story structure of bicycle frames,
    hubcaps, old televisions, toys, bottles and anything else that could be wired together, with rooms, stairs and
    towers you can climb through. The camera roll only has a short video from there, but it is a good one: a tower of
    welded scrap rising out of the vines against that same blue sky, crowned with a metal turbine vent.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cathedral-of-junk.jpg'} alt="A tower of welded scrap metal, pipes and fixtures overgrown with vines, topped by a metal turbine vent, against a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>One of the towers of the Cathedral of Junk.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The Tower</h3>
  <p>
    Late in the afternoon we drove up to the <strong>University of Texas</strong>. UT has one of the most recognizable
    landmarks of any American campus: the 307-foot <strong>Main Building tower</strong>, finished in 1937 to a design by
    Paul Cret. You can see it from much of central Austin, and after a big win its top is lit burnt orange.
  </p>
  <p>
    We came at it the classic way, from the south. A wide boulevard runs straight toward it, ending at the
    <strong>Littlefield Fountain</strong>, a 1933 war memorial by the sculptor Pompeo Coppini, with bronze sea horses
    charging out of the water. Behind the fountain, terraces climb toward the tower under old live oaks. The pool was
    the color of a swimming pool on a postcard, and the beds around it were full of orange flowers.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ut-tower-boulevard.jpg'} alt="The University of Texas tower at the end of a wide boulevard, with the Littlefield Fountain and live oaks in front, under a blue sky with white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The approach from the south, July 1st, 4:30 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The fountain was finished in 1933 and the tower four years later, and from the edge of the pool they line up
    perfectly: the bronze figures in the foreground, the pale tower rising directly behind them, and the dark live oaks
    framing both.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ut-fountain.jpg'} alt="The Littlefield Fountain with its turquoise pool and bronze sculptures, the UT tower rising behind the trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Littlefield Fountain.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ut-main-building.jpg'} alt="The UT Main Building with its tall tower above a wide paved mall, under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Main Building, from the South Mall.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Up close, the tower stands on top of the Main Building like a chess piece on a pedestal. On a Saturday afternoon in
    summer, the South Mall in front of it was empty: wide pale paving, a long strip of brick down the middle, the trees
    throwing hard black shadows, and not a soul on it. From the shade of the building's arcade, one arch framed the whole view back down the mall:
    the paving, the trees, the clouds, and downtown in the distance. Out on University Avenue, the street lines up on
    the dome of the <strong>Texas Capitol</strong>, finished in 1888 in red granite and, as Texans like to point out,
    taller than the one in Washington.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ut-arch.jpg'} alt="A dark stone archway framing a sunlit paved mall, trees and clouds, with downtown Austin in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The South Mall through the Main Building's arcade.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ut-gardens-capitol.jpg'} alt="A round flower garden with orange zinnias at the edge of campus, with the Texas Capitol dome visible at the end of the avenue." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A garden on University Avenue, with the Capitol at the end of the street.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>The River Walk at dusk</h3>
  <p>
    Then we got back on I-35 and drove eighty miles south to <strong>San Antonio</strong>, arriving a little after six.
    San Antonio is the oldest of the triangle's big cities: it was founded around a Spanish mission in 1718, and the
    Alamo is a few blocks from the river. From a terrace above the river, the skyline was backlit by the
    evening sun: a big slab of a hotel, an old Gothic office tower, a church spire, and a sky streaked with thin
    cloud.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sa-skyline.jpg'} alt="The downtown San Antonio skyline backlit by the evening sun, with a church spire and old towers, seen from a terrace." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>San Antonio, backlit, from above the river.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The <strong>River Walk</strong> is the city's great trick. A century ago the San Antonio River was a flood-prone
    ditch that the city wanted to pave over. An architect named Robert Hugman proposed instead to turn the bend downtown
    into a sunken garden of paths, bridges and shops one story below the street, and in 1939–41 the Works Progress
    Administration built it. More branches were added for the 1968 World's Fair and later, and one of them, a short
    loop of river dug in the 1980s, runs right into a shopping mall, so the tour boats float past the food court.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sa-riverwalk.jpg'} alt="The River Walk winding between trees and stone paths below a glass-fronted building." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The River Walk, a story below the street.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sa-bridge.jpg'} alt="A footbridge and a road bridge crowded with people over the dark river, between tall buildings." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Bridges on bridges.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Down at water level it is a different city: shaded by trees, lined with restaurant patios and umbrellas,
    and on a holiday Saturday crowded with people strolling in both directions. The flat, open tour barges kept sliding
    by, packed with passengers. Footbridges arch overhead, and people lean on the railings to watch the boats, while the
    people in the boats look up and watch them back.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sa-boat.jpg'} alt="A yellow-green tour boat full of passengers passing under an arched footbridge on the River Walk, in front of a glass-fronted mall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A tour barge rounds the bend by the mall.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    We stayed on the river until after half past eight, when the light went soft and gold and then gray. Back up at
    street level, on the way out, the city had one more surprise: a line of horse-drawn carriages shaped like
    Cinderella's pumpkin coach, outlined in blue and white and pink LED lights, clopping down the avenue in the dusk.
    Then, that same night, it was eighty miles back up I-35 to Austin, the red Charger finally getting to stretch its legs.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sa-carriages.jpg'} alt="Two horse-drawn carriages shaped like pumpkin coaches, lit with blue and white LED lights, on a San Antonio street at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Pumpkin coaches on the street at dusk.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 2: Fort Worth, then Dallas</h2>
  <p>
    The second day was the long side of the triangle: almost two hundred miles up I-35 from Austin to Fort Worth, past
    Waco and the long flat farmland of the Blackland Prairie. The phone stayed in a pocket for most of it. We reached
    the <strong>Fort Worth Stockyards</strong> at about a quarter to seven in the evening.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0702.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0702.jpg'} alt="Map of the route: Day 2: Austin north to the Fort Worth Stockyards, downtown Fort Worth and the Water Gardens, then east on I-30 to Reunion Tower in Dallas." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 2: from Austin to Cowtown, then east into the sunset's afterglow.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Cowtown</h3>
  <p>
    Fort Worth calls itself "Cowtown" and "where the West begins", and the Stockyards are why. After the Civil War, cattle
    drives up the Chisholm Trail passed through here on their way to the railheads in Kansas. Once the railroad came to
    Fort Worth, the city became the place where Texas cattle were bought, sold and shipped, and in the early 1900s
    the big meatpackers Swift and Armour built plants beside the pens. The packing plants are long gone, but the district
    has kept its brick streets, its Mission-style buildings and its wooden cattle pens. The longhorns are still driven
    down Exchange Avenue twice a day, though not at seven in the evening.
  </p>
  <p>
    What we found instead was a different kind of herd: a row of big touring motorcycles lined up across the brick
    paving of Rodeo Plaza, the riders standing around them in the long evening light, and a white stucco building
    behind them wrapped in scaffolding, with a little crowd of onlookers along the curb.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fw-stockyards.jpg'} alt="A row of motorcycles and riders on a brick-paved plaza in the Fort Worth Stockyards, in front of a white building under scaffolding." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Stockyards, 6:53 pm: horsepower of a different kind.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From the Stockyards, North Main Street runs dead straight downhill toward downtown, and from the top of the rise the
    whole skyline lines up at the end of it: a pair of dark twin towers, a cluster of smaller ones, and street lamps
    curving over the empty road. In the low evening light it looked like a model of a city someone had set down on the
    prairie.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fw-main-street.jpg'} alt="The downtown Fort Worth skyline at the end of a long straight street, under a pale blue evening sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown Fort Worth from North Main Street.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The Water Gardens</h3>
  <p>
    Downtown, we went to the <strong>Fort Worth Water Gardens</strong>, which may be the strangest public park in
    Texas. Philip Johnson and John Burgee designed it in 1974 as a sunken landscape of concrete terraces and pools.
    There is a quiet pool, sunk deep among cypress trees, and an aerated pool that sends up a fine mist. The star is the
    <strong>Active Pool</strong>, where water pours down a steep amphitheater of jagged concrete steps into a small
    square basin at the bottom, and you can walk down the stepping stones right into the middle of it. It looks
    like the future as imagined in the 1970s, and Hollywood thought so too: it was a set for the film <em>Logan's
    Run</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    At eight in the evening there were people picking their way down the steps toward the basin. The design is as much
    about sound as sight: down in the pit, the roar of falling water bounces off the concrete walls and the city
    outside disappears.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fw-active-pool.jpg'} alt="Water cascading down steep terraced concrete steps into a small basin at the Fort Worth Water Gardens." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Active Pool at the Water Gardens.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fw-dusk.jpg'} alt="A tall glass tower and downtown Fort Worth buildings at dusk above the trees of the Water Gardens, with dramatic clouds lit by sunset." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown Fort Worth at dusk.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>East on I-30, and Reunion Tower</h3>
  <p>
    Fort Worth and Dallas are only thirty-odd miles apart, joined by I-30, and the space between them has filled in so
    completely that you never really leave town. Halfway across, in Arlington, we passed <strong>Six Flags Over
    Texas</strong>, which opened in 1961 and was the first of the Six Flags parks; the name refers to the six nations
    whose flags have flown over Texas. Then the sky put on a show. The sun had set behind us, and thin, streaky clouds
    overhead turned peach, pink and lavender, combed into long parallel waves as if someone had dragged a brush through
    them.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'i30-sunset.jpg'} alt="Streaky pink and peach sunset clouds over a flat Texas landscape beside the I-30 freeway." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>I-30 near Grand Prairie, 8:40 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    We pulled into Dallas just after nine, and the first thing we saw was <strong>Reunion Tower</strong>. It is the
    city's signature: a 561-foot concrete shaft topped by a geodesic ball, opened in 1978, which is covered in a lattice
    of 259 LED lights that run animated light shows after dark. That night the ball glowed solid red. A few minutes
    later it switched to white and blue. Two days before the Fourth, that seems unlikely to be a coincidence.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dallas-reunion-red.jpg'} alt="Reunion Tower with its ball lit red against a dark blue night sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Reunion Tower, 9:11 pm: red.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dallas-reunion-night.jpg'} alt="Reunion Tower with its ball lit white and blue against the night sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>9:15 pm: white and blue.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The downtown skyline at night is Dallas showing off. Many of its towers are outlined in colored light: one in pink,
    another with a whole facade glowing red, and above all of them the <strong>Bank of America Plaza</strong>, at 72
    stories the tallest building in the city, traced in brilliant green along every edge, like a drawing of a
    skyscraper rather than a skyscraper. From a window that night we could see it all, with the rail lines and the dark
    streets of the West End below.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dallas-night-lights.jpg'} alt="Downtown Dallas towers lit in pink and red beyond a bright green lawn and street lights at night." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown Dallas lights up.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dallas-night-skyline.jpg'} alt="The Dallas skyline at night, with the Bank of America Plaza outlined in green light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Bank of America Plaza in its green outline.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Day 3: Dallas by day, and south to Houston</h2>
  <p>
    In daylight the same view was all glass and sky: the Bank of America Plaza a plain silver-blue slab now, the
    stepped pyramid of Fountain Place beside it, and the old brick of the West End in the foreground. Big white clouds
    were drifting over the city again, as they had every day of the trip.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0703.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0703.jpg'} alt="Map of the route: Day 3: Reunion Tower, Pioneer Plaza and the Eye in downtown Dallas, dim sum in Richardson, then south to POST Houston." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 3: downtown Dallas, dim sum in Richardson, then four hours down I-45.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dallas-skyline-day.jpg'} alt="The downtown Dallas skyline by day, with glass towers, older brick buildings and rail lines in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown Dallas, July 3rd, late morning.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By noon we were back at the foot of Reunion Tower, which by day turns out to be an unexpectedly elegant object:
    a slim cylinder of concrete with a cage of white struts around the ball, rising out of a construction site with
    a yellow crane parked at its base.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'dallas-reunion-day.jpg'} alt="Reunion Tower by day against a sky of puffy white clouds, rising above a glass building." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Reunion Tower, noon.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>A cattle drive downtown</h3>
  <p>
    A few blocks east, in the middle of the city, is a cattle drive. <strong>Pioneer Plaza</strong> holds 49 bronze
    longhorn steers and three cowboys on horseback, by the sculptor Robert Summers, installed in the 1990s. The steers
    are larger than life and spread out across a hillside of dry grass, coming down a slope, splashing through a
    little stream and climbing out the other side, as if they had been driven up the Shawnee Trail and just kept going
    straight through downtown. It is supposedly the largest bronze monument of its kind in the world. Behind them, the
    mirrored glass of the office towers reflects the clouds, and the effect is pleasantly absurd: a herd from the 1850s
    in front of a wall of mirrored glass from the 1980s.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dallas-pioneer-plaza.jpg'} alt="Bronze longhorn steers walking across dry grass at Pioneer Plaza, with mirrored glass towers behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Pioneer Plaza: the herd heads for the office towers.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dallas-longhorns.jpg'} alt="A line of bronze longhorns crossing a pond in front of glass buildings and trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Crossing the creek.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The grass under the steers was baked to the color of straw. Half an hour later we were among the streets
    north of the plaza, which in downtown Dallas are narrow canyons between old brick office buildings and new glass. Off Main Street, in a garden
    lawn between two black-glass towers, we found the city's most unsettling piece of public art: <em>Eye</em>, by Tony
    Tasset, a thirty-foot fiberglass eyeball, complete with red veins, lying in the grass and staring straight at you.
    It is impossible to stand in front of it without feeling watched, which is presumably the point.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dallas-main-street.jpg'} alt="A narrow downtown Dallas street between old brick and stone office buildings, with a tall tower at the far end." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A downtown street canyon.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dallas-eye.jpg'} alt="A giant sculpture of an eyeball lying on a lawn between glass skyscrapers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Tony Tasset's <em>Eye</em>, watching.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Dim sum in Richardson</h3>
  <p>
    Before heading south, we drove fifteen minutes north to Richardson, a suburb with one of the larger Chinese
    communities in North Texas, for dim sum at <strong>Kirin Court</strong>. After a weekend of barbecue,
    the table filled up with bamboo steamers: soup dumplings with their little twisted tops,
    shumai, steamed spare ribs, chicken feet in a dark glossy sauce, a plate of char siu, baked barbecue-pork buns,
    rice-noodle rolls, a trio of egg tarts, and a big white pot of tea in the middle of it all.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'richardson-dim-sum.jpg'} alt="A table of dim sum: bamboo steamers of soup dumplings, shumai, spare ribs and chicken feet, char siu, baked buns, egg tarts and a white teapot." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dim sum in Richardson.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Houston after dark</h3>
  <p>
    Then came the longest drive of the trip: about four hours down I-45 from Dallas to Houston, through the pine woods of
    East Texas, mostly in the heat of the afternoon, and again almost no photos. We got to <strong>Houston</strong> after
    dark and went straight to an old post office.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>POST Houston</strong> is what happens when a city decides not to tear down a giant concrete box. It was the
    city's main post office, built in 1962 and later named for Barbara Jordan, a sprawling mail-sorting building next to
    the rail lines on the edge of downtown. After the mail moved out, it was reborn in 2021, redesigned by the
    architecture firm OMA as a food hall, a concert venue and, on top, a huge rooftop park and farm. Downstairs in the
    market there was a glass freezer case of paletas, Mexican ice pops, in rows of every color: lime, coconut,
    chocolate and many more, all neatly labeled on little chalkboard signs.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'houston-paletas.jpg'} alt="A glass freezer case full of colorful paletas, Mexican ice pops, with small chalkboard flavor labels." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Paletas in the POST market hall.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Up on the roof, the building's long facade glowed red behind a screen of vertical fins. It was a Houston July night, and the rooftop was dotted with people sitting at
    tables in the dark. Beyond the garden beds, the whole Houston skyline was lit up across the freeway.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'houston-post.jpg'} alt="The long POST Houston building glowing red behind vertical fins at night, with the downtown Houston skyline beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>POST Houston, 9 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'houston-post-roof.jpg'} alt="The rooftop garden of POST Houston at night, with plants, lights and the downtown skyline behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The rooftop garden.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Houston's skyline is the biggest of the four cities, and from up here it is one long wall of light: glass towers
    glowing white, gold and violet, and among them the stepped and peaked tops of the skyscrapers that oil money built.
    It was the night of July 3rd, and the city felt like it was waiting for the next day.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'houston-skyline-night.jpg'} alt="The downtown Houston skyline at night from the POST Houston roof, with towers lit in white, gold and purple." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown Houston from the roof, the night before the Fourth.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 4: Independence Day</h2>
  <p>
    The Fourth of July began with a long lunch downtown, near Discovery Green, at <strong>Xochi</strong>, a restaurant
    devoted to the cooking of Oaxaca from the Houston chef Hugo Ortega. A small wooden card on the table explained
    the name: <em>xochitl</em> is the Nahuatl word for flower.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0704.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0704.jpg'} alt="Map of the route: Day 4: lunch in downtown Houston, Space Center Houston, then the airport." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 4: lunch downtown, the shuttle, and the airport.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The food was the kind that is a little too pretty to eat. A tortilla came topped with dark, glossy braised meat,
    green salsa and pink pickled onion on a big blue-gray plate. A black bowl held something diced, raw and red in a
    yellow broth, dotted with avocado and slivers of radish.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'xochi-taco.jpg'} alt="A tortilla topped with dark braised meat, green salsa and pickled onion on a blue-gray plate." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The first plate.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'xochi-aguachile.jpg'} alt="A dark textured bowl holding a raw diced dish in yellow broth topped with avocado and radish." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Something raw, bright and sour.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Then came a fillet of fish in a bright green sauce with radishes, carrots and green beans, with a pair of little
    tostadas piled with greens alongside. Last came the most theatrical plate: something long, orange-red and glazed,
    which looked for all the world like a whole roasted sweet potato, laid on a gray plate and ringed with dots of
    nearly black sauce like a clock face.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'xochi-fish.jpg'} alt="A seared fish fillet in green sauce with radishes, carrots and green beans, with two small tostadas behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fish in green sauce.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'xochi-dessert.jpg'} alt="A long orange-red glazed dish on a gray plate, ringed with dots of dark sauce, next to a card reading Xochi." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The grand finale.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Independence, on Independence Day</h3>
  <p>
    After lunch we drove half an hour southeast to Clear Lake and <strong>Space Center Houston</strong>, the visitor
    center of NASA's Johnson Space Center. Johnson is the home of Mission Control and of the astronaut corps, which is
    why the first words radioed back from the Moon in 1969 began with "Houston". It was a gray, muggy afternoon, and clouds were
    piling up over the Gulf.
  </p>
  <p>
    You see it from the parking lot: a full-size Boeing 747 parked beside a tall steel tower, with a space shuttle
    riding piggyback on its back. The 747 is the real thing, <strong>NASA 905</strong>, one of the two Shuttle Carrier
    Aircraft that ferried the orbiters across the country, and the plane that carried <em>Enterprise</em> aloft for its
    first glide tests in 1977. The shuttle on top is a high-fidelity replica, and its name is <em>Independence</em>.
    We hadn't planned it, but there are worse ways to spend the Fourth of July than standing under a space shuttle called
    <em>Independence</em>. The exhibit, called Independence Plaza, opened in 2016, and you can climb the tower and walk
    through both the plane and the shuttle.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'space-shuttle.jpg'} alt="A space shuttle replica mounted on top of a white Boeing 747 carrier aircraft beside a tall steel tower, under gray clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption><em>Independence</em> on NASA 905, July 4th, 2:25 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Inside, the main hall is a big dark hangar of a room, its ceiling hung with a grid of lights, the floor polished
    so that everything is reflected twice. It was crowded with holiday families. In one corner hung a full-size
    <strong>Apollo lunar module</strong>, the spidery gold-foil lander that set twelve astronauts down on the Moon, its
    landing legs splayed over the crowd. A sign beyond it pointed to the Starship Gallery. Next to it, a red steel
    gantry climbed toward the ceiling.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'space-hall.jpg'} alt="The main hall of Space Center Houston, a dark hangar-like space with a lit sign, exhibits and crowds of visitors." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The main hall, Space Center Houston.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'space-lunar.jpg'} alt="An Apollo lunar module suspended in the hall, with a red steel gantry staircase beside it and crowds below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A lunar module, landing on the crowd.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    And that, as far as the camera roll is concerned, was the trip. The last trace of it is a short video from the road
    outside the terminals at George Bush Intercontinental, IAH, at a quarter past eight on the evening of the Fourth,
    with the light fading over the airport.
  </p>

  <h2>Four days, five cities</h2>
  <p>
    The Texas Triangle is not, on paper, a romantic road trip. It is mostly interstate: I-35 north through the prairie,
    I-30 across the Metroplex, I-45 south through the pines. The cities at its corners are big, hot and spread out, and
    you spend a lot of time looking at them through a windshield.
  </p>
  <p>
    But the four cities turned out to be four completely different characters, each with its own idea of what Texas is.
    Austin is a university town with a capitol at one end of its main street and a cathedral of junk in a backyard.
    San Antonio is old, Spanish and built around water, a city that solved its flooding river by turning it into its
    living room. Fort Worth still dresses like the cattle town it was, and Dallas, half an hour away, dresses like the
    bank it became, with a herd of bronze longhorns walking past the glass. Houston is the biggest and the least
    concerned with how it looks, a city that turns its old post office into a rooftop park and keeps a space shuttle
    out by the bay.
  </p>
  <p>
    And then there is the sky. Almost every photo from this trip taken outdoors in daylight has the same sky in it:
    deep blue, enormous, piled with white clouds. That is the real Texas landmark, and it followed us all the way around
    the triangle. Four days, five cities, about 750 miles in a car too red for its own good, and a shuttle called
    <em>Independence</em> on Independence Day. Not a bad long weekend.
  </p>
